    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.icon\n\n### Description\n\n`mini.icons` assigns the neovim glyph to a file named `init.lua`. However, the name is not unique to neovim configs/plugins, but a standard for the lua language itself for the file of a module. As such, the filename `init.lua` is used in many non-nvim projects, and files such as `~/.config/hammerspoon/init.lua` also get the nvim icon, even though they have nothing to do with neovim.\n\n### Neovim version\n\nNVIM v0.10.2\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nRun `MiniIcons.get(\"file\", \"~/.config/hammerspoon/init.lua\")`\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nOnly use neovim glyph when in an nvim project.\n\n### Actual behavior\n\nalways uses neovim glyph for `init.lua` files",
